== 7. M_ONENARROW'S MECHANISM, PINNED (GLM: a future reorder would otherwise orphan the x86-64 kill silently) ==
  RECORDED POP2 order (frozen rev 3): SADDR2=0x04 SLAVELMOD1=0x08 SLAVELMOD2=0x0c SLAVECTL=0x10 MASTER1=0x14 MASTER2=0x18 MASTER3=0x1c MASTER4=0x20 MASTER4MOD=0x24 MASTERCTL=0x28 DMACTL=0x30 DMAMODE=0x34 DMALADDR=0x38 DMAVMEADDR=0x3c DMACOUNT=0x40 DMATABLE=0x44 DMASTAT=0x48 T2CMP=0x58 T2COUNT=0x5c PRESCALE=0x64 IRQSTAT=0x68 SETSOFTIRQ=0x70 IRQCLR=0x74 IRQL1=0x78 IRQL2=0x7c MISC=0x8c
  RECORDED MISC at 0x8c: latch index 0x8c >> 2 = 35 >= 32; a 32-bit shift whose count is masked to 5 bits
           uses bit 3 -- the bit of SLAVELMOD2 (0x0c >> 2 = 3)
  RECORDED POP2 index of SLAVELMOD2 = 2, of MISC = 25: SLAVELMOD2 is reached FIRST in `p2 ann rd' and `p2 ann wr' (both walk
           POP2 in order), so its first access sets bit 3 and MISC's first access then finds it set -- MISC's
           announce is silenced: 25 lines in each pass 1.  No other population-2 index reaches 32.
  This is the reading-derived prediction for THIS x86-64 build (Fable, grok, GLM).  The shift is UNDEFINED in C,
  so no portable claim is made (Astra, D5): a build whose shift yields 0 would red all four ann rows (MISC never
  latches: 27 lines in pass 1, 2 in pass 2); a build that emits a 64-bit bit-test/set with the unmasked count
  would behave like the fix and SURVIVE.  Table 2 records the compiler, flags, host, the leg's object code for the
  two arm lines (disassembled AFTER the run) and which row reds.

